<!-- SECTION 9: CHALLENGES -->
<section class="content-section" id="challenges-section">
    <div class="content-container">
        <div class="text-content">
            <h2 class="section-title">Types of Turnover in Tender Certificates</h2>
            <div class="content-text">
                <div class="table-responsive-wrapper">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Challenge</th><th>Impact</th><th>How Patron Accounting Solves It</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Total Annual Turnover</td><td>Entire revenue/sales for a FY from all business activities. Most basic requirement. From P&amp;L top-line, cross-verified with ITR and GST.</td><td>Most supply tenders and smaller works tenders require only total turnover. Derived directly from audited financials.</td></tr><tr><td>Average Annual Turnover</td><td>Mean of total turnover for N years: Sum ÷ N. Most common: "at least Rs X crore for last 3 FYs." Some allow "best 3 out of 5."</td><td>CA computes per NIT formula. Bidder selects highest years if "best-of-N" allowed. Certified in prescribed format.</td></tr><tr><td>Similar Works Turnover</td><td>Revenue only from contracts matching tender scope. PMC road tender = revenue from road construction only. Project-wise verification needed.</td><td>Most complex. Work orders + completion certificates + running account bills examined per project. Patron specialises for Pune contractors.</td></tr><tr><td>Minimum Annual (Each Year)</td><td>Some tenders require minimum in each year (not average). One year below threshold = disqualified.</td><td>CA certifies each year separately. Important for businesses with fluctuating turnover to check every year meets threshold.</td></tr><tr><td>% of Estimated Cost</td><td>GEM and PSU tenders: "avg annual turnover of at least 30% (or 50% or 100%) of estimated cost."</td><td>CA certifies actual turnover. Evaluation committee compares against estimated cost. Bidder should verify before bidding.</td></tr>

                    </tbody>
                </table>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

