                <div class="faq-expanded__list">
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-1">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What is turnover certificate for tender?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>CA-certified document specifically prepared for tender participation, confirming bidder's turnover for the FYs and in the format specified in the NIT. Proves minimum financial eligibility. Must carry UDIN. Without it, bid rejected as non-responsive during technical evaluation. Patron prepares NIT-specific certificates for all Pune tender authorities.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-2">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Why is turnover certificate needed for tenders?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Government and PSU authorities need to ensure bidders have sufficient financial capacity to execute the contract. The certificate proves revenue scale and operational capability. Minimum threshold typically linked to estimated contract value (100-200%). All bids evaluated against this threshold before financial bid is opened.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-3">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What is average annual turnover?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Mean of total turnover for specified years. Formula: Sum of annual turnover for N years ÷ N. Example: FY 22-23 Rs 3 Cr + FY 23-24 Rs 5 Cr + FY 24-25 Rs 4 Cr = average (3+5+4)/3 = Rs 4 Cr. Some tenders allow 'best 3 out of 5 years.' CA computes as per NIT formula.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-4">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What is similar works turnover?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Revenue only from contracts matching the tender scope. For PMC road tender: revenue from road construction only (not building/electrical). CA verifies project-wise: examines work orders, checks NIT definition match, confirms revenue from client certificates, certifies aggregate. Most complex certification requiring detailed project documentation.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-5">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What format does the tender require?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Every tender specifies its own. Found in NIT under 'Eligibility Criteria' or 'Annexures.' Many Pune tenders (PMC, PCMC, PWD) include a prescribed annexure the CA must fill exactly. Wrong format or missing declaration leads to bid rejection. Patron ensures every certificate matches NIT format exactly.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-6">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Can MSME bidders get turnover exemption?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Under MSE Procurement Policy 2012, Udyam-registered bidders may get relaxation of prior turnover and experience requirements. Minimum threshold may not apply or be reduced. Still need a turnover certificate. Also get EMD exemption, 25% reservation, 15% price preference. Patron highlights MSME status in every certificate.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-7">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What happens if turnover is insufficient?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Bid rejected as non-responsive. Financial bid never opened. No appeal for turnover shortfall. Options: JV/consortium with qualifying partner (if tender allows), wait and build turnover, target lower-threshold tenders, or MSME-reserved tenders. Patron advises on tender selection based on turnover profile.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-8">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Is UDIN mandatory for tender certificates?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Yes, absolutely. Since February 2019, every CA certificate must carry UDIN. Evaluation committees routinely verify at udin.icai.org. Certificate without UDIN is treated as unverified and rejected. Some NITs explicitly state 'CA certificates without UDIN will not be accepted.' Patron generates UDIN on every certificate.</p></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>


            <!-- Quick Answers -->
            <div class="highlight-box" style="margin-top:32px;">
                <p><strong>Quick Answers</strong></p>
                <p><strong>Tender ke liye turnover certificate kya hai?</strong> CA-certified document - bidder ka turnover NIT ke format mein. UDIN mandatory. Types: total, average annual, similar works. Bina iske bid reject hoga. 3-5 din standard; 1 ghanta urgent.</p><p><strong>Pune mein tender turnover certificate kaise banwayein?</strong> NIT padhein - requirement identify karein. CA ko ITR, GST returns, work orders dein. CA verify karega, NIT format mein certificate banayega, UDIN lagaega. E-tender portal par upload karein. Patron Pune office se 1 ghante mein bhi mil sakta hai.</p><p><strong>Galat format se bid reject ho sakta hai?</strong> Haan - generic format use karne se NIT-prescribed annexure ki jagah technical bid reject hota hai. Always match the exact NIT format.</p>

            </div>
